A connection that could not be made says so, instead of leaving the caller waiting forever.

xpc_connection_create_mach_service against a name nothing serves produced a connection that looked
alive and answered nothing at all. Activation looks the service up, fails, and jumps to error_out,
which released the ports and set activated to false. Nothing else. The mach context is never
connected on that path, so a later send never reaches libdispatch, libdispatch therefore never calls
the async reply handler, and a client that asked for a reply waits for a service that does not exist
until something kills it.

macOS answers XPC_ERROR_CONNECTION_INVALID, promptly, on both channels: the event handler hears it,
and a send with reply completes with it.

MEASURED with a probe that carries its own control, one name that nothing can possibly serve beside
the name under test, because a probe with no control is how the previous instrument lied for two
rungs:

    before   control=TIMEOUT                     com.apple.security.syspolicy=TIMEOUT
    after    control=ERROR(Connection invalid)   com.apple.security.syspolicy=ERROR(Connection invalid)

MoneyMoney is the case that found it. Its only XPC lookup is com.apple.security.syspolicy, nothing
in this prefix serves that name, and the application sat on its own startup screen. It STILL sits
there with this fixed, so this is one defect on the way and not the whole story, but a client that
waits forever for a service that cannot exist is wrong on its own terms.

--- a/src/connection.m
+++ b/src/connection.m
@@ -903,6 +903,22 @@
 	xpc_mach_port_release_send(this->send_port);
 	xpc_mach_port_release_receive(this->recv_port);
 	this->activated = false;
+	/*
+	 * A CONNECTION THAT COULD NOT BE MADE MUST SAY SO, and until now it said nothing at all.
+	 *
+	 * The mach context is never connected on this path, so a later send never reaches libdispatch
+	 * and libdispatch therefore never calls the async reply handler. A client that asked for a
+	 * reply from a service that does not exist waited forever. macOS delivers
+	 * XPC_ERROR_CONNECTION_INVALID: the event handler hears it here, and sendMessage:queue:withReply:
+	 * answers with it rather than sending into nothing.
+	 *
+	 * MoneyMoney is the case that found this. It looks up com.apple.security.syspolicy, which
+	 * nothing in this prefix serves, and then sat on its own startup screen forever.
+	 */
+	this->activation_failed = true;
+	if (this->event_handler) {
+		this->event_handler(XPC_ERROR_CONNECTION_INVALID);
+	}
 }
 
 - (BOOL)activate
@@ -1027,6 +1043,18 @@
 {
 	@autoreleasepool {
 		XPC_THIS_DECL(connection);
+
+		/* See activateLocked: a connection whose service never resolved has no route for a reply,
+		 * so answer the caller instead of leaving it waiting. */
+		if (this->activation_failed) {
+			xpc_handler_t copied = Block_copy(handler);
+
+			dispatch_async(queue ? queue : dispatch_get_main_queue(), ^{
+				copied(XPC_ERROR_CONNECTION_INVALID);
+				Block_release(copied);
+			});
+			return;
+		}
 		XPC_CLASS(serializer)* serializer = [XPC_CLASS(serializer) serializer];
 		xpc_connection_reply_context_t* context = NULL;
 		dispatch_mach_msg_t message = NULL;
--- a/internal-include/xpc/objects/connection.h
+++ b/internal-include/xpc/objects/connection.h
@@ -75,6 +75,11 @@
 	//
 	pthread_rwlock_t activated_lock;
 	bool activated;
+	/* SET WHEN ACTIVATION FAILED, which for a named client means the service could not be looked
+	 * up. Without it a send goes to a mach context that was never connected, libdispatch never
+	 * calls the async reply handler, and the caller waits FOREVER for a service that does not
+	 * exist. macOS answers XPC_ERROR_CONNECTION_INVALID instead. */
+	bool activation_failed;
 	pthread_rwlock_t server_peers_lock;
 	xpc_genarr_connection_t server_peers;
 	pthread_rwlock_t remote_credentials_lock;
